$DATUM 123-91-1
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General Procedure for Stille Cross-Coupling reaction Using Ligand 1
(Procedure A). In a glove box, the Schlenk tube equipped with a stir bar
was charged with Pd2(dba)3 (11.2 mg, 0.012 mmol, 1.5 mol %) CsF (272 mg,
1.8 mmol). The tube was fitted with a rubber septum and removed out of
the glove box. One mL of 1,4-dioxane, stock solution of ligand 1 in
dioxane (16.8 mg, 0.049 mmol, 6 mol %), aryl chloride (1.0 equiv, aryl
chloride if solid was added inside the glove box), and organotin reagent
(1.1 equiv) were added successively using syringes. The tube was sealed
with a Teflon screwcap. The reaction mixture was stirred for 30 minutes
at room temperature then heated with stirring at the indicated
temperature for the indicated time. The reaction mixture was cooled to
room temperature, diluted with ether or acetone, filtered, and
concentrated in vacuo. The crude product was purified by column
chromatography on silica gel. Methyl 4-vinylbenzoate6 (Table 1, entry 1).
Procedure A was followed using methyl 4-chlorobenzoate (140 mg, 0.816
mmol) and vinyltributyltin (0.27 mL, 0.898 mmol). The reaction mixture
was purified by column chromatography on silica gel (1% ethyl
acetate/hexanes) to afford 123 mg (93%) of the desired product as a white
solid. 1H NMR (300 MHz, CDCl3): δ 7.99 (d, J = 8.4 Hz, 2H), 7.45 (d, J =
8.4 Hz, 2H), 6.74 (dd, J =17.5, 11.0 Hz, 1H ), 5.86 (d, J = 17.5 Hz, 1H),
5.38 (d, J = 11.0 Hz, 1H), 3.90 (s, 3H). 13C NMR (75.5 MHz, CDCl3): δ
167.1, 142.1, 136.2, 130.1, 129.5, 126.3, 116.7, 52.3.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM Stille cross-coupling reaction
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Highly active palladium catalysts supported by bulky proazaphosphatrane
ligands for Stille cross-coupling: Coupling of aryl and vinyl chlorides,
room temperature coupling of aryl bromides, coupling of aryl triflates,
and synthesis of sterically hindered biaryls
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
$DATUM Su, Weiping; Urgaonkar, Sameer; McLaughlin, Patrick A.; Verkade, John G.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):CITATION
$DATUM Journal of the American Chemical Society (2004), 126(50), 16433-16439

